	<h2>Your Trusted CDCP Provider in Surrey</h2>
	<p>
		The <strong>Canadian Dental Care Plan (CDCP)</strong> is a fantastic government initiative designed to lower dental costs for eligible Canadians. At Canary Dental Clinic, we are proud to be an officially registered CDCP provider, ensuring that our community receives the high-quality care they deserve without the financial stress.
	</p>

	<h3>What Services Are Covered Under CDCP?</h3>
	<p>
		The CDCP covers a wide range of essential dental services. Depending on your eligibility, the plan may cover:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Preventative Care:</strong> Exams, X-rays, and <a href="/dental-cleaning">professional dental cleanings</a>.</li>
		<li><strong>Restorative Services:</strong> Dental fillings to treat cavities.</li>
		<li><strong>Oral Surgery:</strong> Tooth extractions, including <a href="/wisdom-tooth-extraction">wisdom tooth removal</a>.</li>
		<li><strong>Endodontics:</strong> Root canal treatments for infected teeth.</li>
		<li><strong>Periodontics:</strong> Deep scaling for gum disease.</li>
	</ul>

	<h3>How to Use Your CDCP Benefits at Canary Dental</h3>
	<ol>
		<li><strong>Step 1: Check Eligibility.</strong> Ensure you have received your welcome letter and Sun Life CDCP card.</li>
		<li><strong>Step 2: Book Your Appointment.</strong> Contact our clinic. Let us know you are a CDCP patient.</li>
		<li><strong>Step 3: Bring Your Documents.</strong> Bring your Sun Life CDCP card and a valid piece of ID to your appointment.</li>
		<li><strong>Step 4: Receive Care.</strong> We bill Sun Life directly for covered services. Before treatment, ask what you may owe, including any co-payment, charges above the CDCP fee, or services the plan doesn’t cover.</li>
	</ol>

	<h3>Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
	<details>
		<summary>Does the CDCP cover the full cost of my visit?</summary>
		<p>Your co-payment for eligible services is 0%, 40%, or 60% of the CDCP fee, depending on your adjusted family net income. Even with a 0% co-payment, you may pay the difference if the clinic charges more than the CDCP fee, or pay for services the plan doesn’t cover. Ask for an estimate before treatment. See <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/services/benefits/dental/dental-care-plan/coverage.html">Canada.ca’s coverage and cost guide</a>.</p>
	</details>
	<details>
		<summary>Can I qualify for the CDCP if my employer offers dental coverage?</summary>
		<p>Generally, no. If you have access to private dental coverage through your employer, you’re generally not eligible for the CDCP, even if you choose not to enrol. Coverage through a government social program is different: you may still qualify. Check <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/services/benefits/dental/dental-care-plan/qualify.html">Canada.ca for the full eligibility rules</a> or read our <a href="/blog/cdcp-vs-private-insurance-surrey">comparison of the CDCP and private dental insurance</a>.</p>
	</details>

	<h3>Ready to Book?</h3>
	<p>
